From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: retroforge <retroforge@invalid>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 10:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(network): a join keeps one working profile, Connected
 means activated, networks can be forgotten

Joining a network from Settings > Network saved a password one character
longer than the real one, and the UI had no way to undo it (HW, MANGMI
Air X, 2026-10-18). What NetworkManager stored was exactly what the dialog
held: the dialog still held those 15 characters hours later. Causes:

 - The password field was never cleared. It kept what was typed for the
   last dialog, behind dots, so a mistyped password was submitted again on
   every later try; and nothing showed what had been typed.
 - Every join added a profile (the core's connect() always calls
   AddConnection) next to the ones already saved, so a corrected password
   sat beside the wrong one, and a failed join left its profile for
   NetworkManager to retry in the background. There was no way to
   forget a network.
 - "Connected" was shown for the access point NetworkManager was only
   trying (it becomes the active one at the first association attempt),
   and a join could read the previous connection's ACTIVATED right after
   the call.
 - A dialog left without submitting kept its one-shot submit handler, so
   the next Submit also joined that earlier network with the new password.
 - After NetworkManager restarted, the core instance had missed the
   devices exported in its first moments (it knew only the Wi-Fi P2P
   device), and joins had no effect until OGUI was restarted.

The dialog now opens empty and hidden, with a Show password toggle, B
closes it, and a saved network can be forgotten (all its profiles are
deleted, through nmcli: the core has no call for it). A join is followed
by its own activation state and keeps exactly one profile: the new one
replaces the old ones once it works, and is deleted if it does not. The
status column says Connected only for an activated device. The
NetworkManager node replaces an instance that lost its Wi-Fi device. The
first-boot network screen gets the same field and submit fixes.
